{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310819,"nonce":"n9tXQmL66gH0vExUyyFmmmT2Gf0=","title":"WASM llama decode is ~8.7x slower than native (9 vs 78 tok/s on M1) and the gap is the host parallel matmul reading weights from the WASM ...","message":"**Measured 2026-06-14 (M1 Pro, Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M, correctly-built binary):**\nnative decode 78 tok/s, wasm decode 9 tok/s = **8.7x gap**. Single-threaded\n(RAYZOR_WORKERS=1): native 36 vs wasm 2.99 = **12x per-thread**. Parallel\nscaling is similar on both (~2x from 2→7 lanes; memory-bound). RAYZOR_WORKERS\u003e8\nis catastrophic on wasm (oversubscription: 10→1.66 tok/s).\n\n**Profiling (RAYZOR_WASM_MM_PROFILE=1 dump added to host_parallel_qmatmul in\ncompiler/src/codegen/wasm_runner.rs, gated, uncommitted):** over an 80-token\ndecode, host_qmatmul fanout = **7631ms of 8322ms wall = 91.7%**; prequant\n(f32→Q8_K on the caller) = 11.7ms (negligible). So the matmul IS the wasm\nbottleneck and the in-wasm non-matmul rest is only ~8%.\n\n**Root cause — NOT the kernel, NOT threading, NOT scalar fallback:**\n- The one-shot probe confirmed host_qmatmul uses **SDOT** (dotprod IS enabled\n  for the compiler crate via `[target.aarch64-apple-darwin] target-feature=+dotprod`).\n- Native's `qmatmul_chunk_impl_sdot_q4km` (runtime/src/quant.rs:1809-1822,\n  default `use_llamacpp` path) runs the **IDENTICAL** per-block\n  `dot_q4_k_q8_kblock_llamacpp` loop that host_parallel_qmatmul\n  (wasm_runner.rs ~705-727) reimplements. Same primitive, same Q4_K_M\n  stride/layout, same loop.\n- Dispatch config has no lever: caller-assist HELPS (8.22 w/o vs 9.07 w/),\n  stealing is marginal (STATIC_BANDS 9.32), 7 workers saturate 8 P-cores.\n- The worker threads are pure-native in BOTH paths. The ONLY difference is\n  native reads the Q4_K_M weights from a native RayzorQTensor allocation;\n  the wasm host reads them from the **wasm linear memory** (`base + qt_data_off`,\n  ~0.7GB streamed per token). That read is ~12x slower per thread.\n\n**NAME IS A MISNOMER — it is NOT memory-bound.** Every concrete hypothesis was\nREFUTED by direct measurement (correctly-built binary, 80-tok decode):\n- **Memory: REFUTED.** A/B copied the weight matrix to a native `Vec` and dotted\n  against THAT (`RAYZOR_WASM_MM_NATIVE_W=1`, copy outside the fanout timer):\n  fanout 5794ms vs 5762ms baseline — IDENTICAL. So the wasm-memory weight reads\n  are NOT slower than native reads.\n- **opt-level: REFUTED.** Moved the per-band loop into the runtime crate\n  (opt-level=3, vs the compiler crate's opt-level=2 LLVM-bug workaround) AND\n  added `#[inline(never)]` to stop thin-LTO re-inlining it back to opt-level=2:\n  fanout 7386ms vs 7652ms — no change.\n- **kernel: REFUTED.** Native `qmatmul_chunk_impl_sdot_q4km` runs the IDENTICAL\n  `dot_q4_k_q8_kblock_llamacpp` per-block loop; SDOT confirmed by a cfg probe.\n- **threading: REFUTED.** Same poor scaling on both (~2x from 2→7 lanes); the\n  gap is PER-LANE (12x single-thread).\nAll the above were reverted to a clean tree.\n\n**Actual remaining cause:** the SAME opt-level=3 native worker threads running\nthe SAME compiled function over the SAME-speed memory are ~12x slower PER LANE\nwhen the matmul is dispatched from inside a wasmtime host call vs from a native\nprocess. That points at an OS/CPU-level effect of the wasmtime process —\ncandidates: macOS scheduling the wasmtime process onto E-cores (the worker-pool\nQoS `USER_INTERACTIVE` hint may be overridden at the process level), CPU\nfrequency throttling, or cache/TLB pollution from the wasmtime runtime + the\n4 GiB reservation. NEEDS OS-LEVEL PROFILING: `powermetrics --samplers cpu_power`\n(core residency + freq) while the wasm decode runs, or `RAYZOR_QOS`/explicit\nthread-affinity experiments. Until that's understood, NO code-level matmul fix\nwill move the needle.\n\n**EVIDENCE 2026-06-14 (correctly-built binary):**\n- `/usr/bin/time -l` on a 60-tok wasm decode: 17.14 real / **55.46 user** / 2.39 sys.\n  The decode (~7.9s real) runs ~6 cores in parallel BUT burns ~8.7x more\n  CPU-SECONDS than native for the same 60 tokens. Workers ARE running; they are\n  ~8.7x slower per core. Machine = 8 P-cores + 2 E-cores.\n- `taskpolicy` (no sudo, clamps QoS down only): default 9.27 tok/s, `-c utility`\n  7.8, `-c maintenance` 0.36, `-b` (background) 0.34. So QoS/core assignment\n  hugely matters, but the baseline is ALREADY at the worker pool's\n  USER_INTERACTIVE max — taskpolicy can't raise it, and native at the SAME QoS\n  is 78. So the worker QoS hint may not be STICKING in the wasmtime context, OR\n  the P-cores are stalling.\n- The 8.7x is compute (SDOT), which is register-bound after the block read, so a\n  pure-frequency (E-core, ~3x) story is incomplete — needs the actual P/E\n  residency + frequency.\n\n**THE NEXT MEASUREMENT (needs sudo — user runs it):**\n```\ncd nue/examples/llama-chat\nSIDE=$REPO/target/wasm32-wasip1-threads-pic/release/nue_plugins.wasm\nRAYZOR_KV_Q8=1 RAYZOR_Q8_SIDE_MODULE=$SIDE RAYZOR_WASM_NO_WGPU=1 \\\n  $REPO/target/release/rayzor run --wasm Main.hx --no-cache -- $MODEL \"\u003clong prompt\u003e\" 2000 4096 0.0\nsudo powermetrics --samplers cpu_power -i 1000 -n 10 | grep -iE 'cluster|active residency|frequency'\n```\n**POWERMETRICS RESULT (user ran it, 2026-06-14) — E-CORE/THROTTLE REFUTED:**\nDuring a wasm decode, BOTH P-clusters are at **100% active residency, 3036–3228\nMHz (full top-bin boost)**; all 8 P-cores (CPU 2–9) ~92–98% active, every one\npegged at 3228 MHz. E-cores (CPU 0–1) only ~50–90% at 1.6–2.0 GHz. So the\nworkers ARE on the P-cores at full frequency, ~95% busy — NOT E-cores, NOT\nthrottled, NOT idle. Yet throughput is 8.7x lower. **=\u003e the P-cores are busy\nbut UNPRODUCTIVE — STALLING, not running slow.** Spin-wait pool ruled out too\n(condvar pool measured the same ~9 tok/s).\n\nSo the matmul stalls ~8.7x more on full-speed P-cores in the wasmtime process,\nfor the identical SDOT kernel. Candidates left: cache/TLB misses (the 4 GiB\nlinear-memory mapping + the F32-dequant footprint vs native's scattered heap),\nor false-sharing / cross-context coherency between the wasm guest thread and the\nnative worker pool dispatched from inside the host call. **NEXT = Instruments\n(needs GUI/sudo): \"CPU Counters\" template, watch L1D/L2 cache-miss rate, TLB\nmisses, and backend-stall cycles for the worker threads during a wasm decode vs\na native decode.** That names the stall. Likely fixes once known: huge pages /\nMAP_POPULATE for the wasm memory; stop the GGUFLoader F32-dequant (keep weights\nQ4 so the resident footprint is ~0.7GB not ~6GB — Main.hx:184 flags this as a\n\"currently\" hack); or the in-wasm wasi_thread_spawn endgame to avoid the\nguest→host cross-context dispatch entirely.\n\n**Architectural note:** the host parallel matmul ([[perf_wasm_host_parallel_matmul]])\ngives only 1.9x over the in-wasm SIMD128 path because BOTH run in the wasmtime\nprocess and hit this same per-lane wall. The endgame (in-wasm wasi_thread_spawn)\nwould also run guest-side, so it likely hits the same wall — confirm the OS/CPU\nroot cause FIRST.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_wasm_matmul_memory_bound` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}